  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981801/assets/e97552a634b4e3ad3604de9d75742af05aa30a18ee92c8440e6c3cedd5d7273a.png)\n\nConsider the function $y=f(x)$ whose graph is plotted above. On which of the following intervals is the extreme value theorem applicable to $f(x)?$\n\n- $[-4,-1]$\n- $[0,4]$\n- $[1,6]$",
  "worked_solution": "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous. \n\nWith that in mind, let's take a look at each interval.\n\n- The interval $[-4,-1]$ is closed, but the function has a removable discontinuity at $x=-2.$  Therefore, the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- The interval $[0,4]$ is closed, but the function is not defined at $x=0.$ So, it can't be continuous at this point. Therefore, the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- The interval $[1,6]$ is closed, and the function is continuous on $[1,6].$ Therefore, the extreme value theorem is applicable.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"III only\".",
